Z||000001||PBJ||06-12-2001||09:10 PM||||The only thing you are changing with tilt for ignition switch is a mechanical link. wires for ignition switch are much lower and are not affected by tilt.<P>If you access your electrical switch mounted on top of steering column under dash you will find it is adjustable and may have come out of adjustment. same bolts that adjust switch also effect high beam switch.<P>Could be your ignition switch rack assembly within the steering column is worn out but I have never seen this on fiero style column-only on corsica and beretta columns.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||06-12-2001||09:37 PM||||  Thanks PBJ, That's definitely a place to start. Also noticed that I have to "over turn" the switch. So maybe the mechanical link is the culprit. Anyone else?? Thanks<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||06-13-2001||04:05 PM||||  Ok, Well the problem got worse, way worse! I pulled the steering wheel and got the lock cylandder out. It's in good shape. I found the mechanical linkage and was able to move it for and aft with my finger. Now I have the whole upper section (north of the tilt) torn apart. Well, actually,, it sprung apart. <BR>  I am convinced that the problem is in the linkage at or around the tilt knuckle. The only problem I have is; how in the hell do I get to it? I can't seem to figure out the tilt handle   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  and how to disconnect the turnsignal harness. It's probably just frustration, but man I am at witts end. <BR>  Does anyone have an exploded view of the steering column? Like what would be in the factory manual? I need more info than my Haynes or Chiltons manuals offer. Any help is appriciated.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 06-13-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000004||thomas_l||06-13-2001||05:51 PM||||The switch has a sliding adjustment. It is located at the lower end of the column right at the end of the shroud. Normally the tilt has no effect. The linkage driven by the lock cylinder presses a straight rod with a curved end. The only room for slip would be the switch down at the end of the rod. I guess if the bolts holding the pivot were loose (i.e. steering wheel and upper column "flop" around) that could cause the same - but I've never seen that occur.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||06-13-2001||11:53 PM||||  Thomas, I did readjust  the mechanism, and it worked pretty good for a minute, Then the lock cylinder started turning freely. Tor the whole thing apart and found that when I turn the key, the upper part of the mechanism (above the pivot) moved with the key, the lower part (attached to the switch) remained stationary. There has to be a mechanical connection that came loose or broke. I do not have the right mauals to discern the acutal problem. An exploded view of the column would help greatly. I will probably have to replace the upper housing as some pieces flew out when it seperatedd. Also, how in the world do you remove the tilt handle? TIA<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
